INT. FUNERAL PARLOUR - SIDE ROOM - DAY

Tapestried chairs. A sideboard of sherry glasses. Five people stand in a half circle around a closed casket.

DOROTHY WAKEFIELD, 70s, the widow. VICTOR WAKEFIELD, 40s, her son. TABITHA CROWE, 30s, the nurse. LEONTYNE BASS, 50s, the lawyer. FATHER OSWALD QUINN, 60s, the priest.

DOROTHY
He didn't fall.

VICTOR
Mother.

DOROTHY
He didn't fall, Victor. He was pushed.

TABITHA
Mrs. Wakefield, the coroner said--

DOROTHY
The coroner said what he was paid to say.

LEONTYNE
Dorothy, I advise you not to make accusations in front of witnesses.

DOROTHY
Witnesses to what? Five people, one dead man, and a staircase.

FATHER QUINN
Dorothy, grief makes a person see monsters.

DOROTHY
Father, forty years I have heard you talk of mercy. Tell me, where was mercy at two in the morning on Sunday?

VICTOR
I was home.

TABITHA
I was on shift.

LEONTYNE
I was at my club.

FATHER QUINN
I was at prayers.

DOROTHY
And I was asleep. So who was it?

Silence. The clock ticks. A sherry glass trembles on the sideboard.

TABITHA
(very quietly)
He called me at one-forty. Said he'd heard someone in the house.

VICTOR
You never told us.

TABITHA
I didn't think it mattered.

DOROTHY
Who did he say it was?

TABITHA
He said it was someone from this room.
